module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.one_type_variable_bound_to_two_distinct_coproducts

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BlockerClassUnstated, RecurringFailureMode, RungUnread }

data one_type_variable_bound_to_two_distinct_coproducts: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "one_type_variable_bound_to_two_distinct_coproducts" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungUnread { cause: BlockerClassUnstated },

  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: a generic fn `same_arm<T>(a: T, b: T)` is called with one argument of coproduct Pa and the other of a DIFFERENT coproduct Pb, so one type variable T is bound to two distinct declarations. It compiles and runs, answering false. (An `==` over `ConstructorTag<T>` against `ConstructorTag<U>` was probed earlier in the session but no probe for it is enrolled, so it is NOT claimed here.) This is DESIGN section 4b's compiler floor, `values inhabit declared types`, not a property of any carrier.",
    "DISTINGUISHING FACTS (measured by quiet-hawk-507, retained through the 10-20 merge re-home): a non-generic f(a: P) called with Q1 is refused, and constructor expressions already elaborate to the owner coproduct. The hole is generic rebind, not variant-to-coproduct typing.",
    "RECEIPT (executed 2026-10-14, remote runner, main b69fc48027 plus the probe modules cited as evidence, `gunbc run --claim-run`): v1 route (--source-root dag) and v2 route (--source-root src/v2) BOTH print `FAIL same_arm_one_t_two_coproducts_must_refuse`, a false verdict from a run that should have been a typecheck refusal. The same-coproduct controls pass on both routes: five PASS over a 12-variant payload-bearing coproduct, so the probe is not failing for an unrelated reason.",
    "HARM: a silent wrong answer wherever a typed capability would be built on a generic parameter. A constructor-tag carrier keyed by T cannot refuse comparing a tag of one coproduct with a tag of another, which is the property that makes it better than a raw String discriminant. It is the hand-written mirror's own failure at scale: on a held branch of #14009 a mirror equated AttestationValidationCategoryAbsent with AttestationNonceMismatch, and nothing refused it.",
    "RUNG FOUND AT: below mitigatable for this shape: the program is accepted and answers falsely. ACCEPTANCE LOCATED (receipt: quiet-hawk-507's locate probes, run after the b69fc48027 run below, which stays as the historical receipt that both routes accept): v1 `v1.compiler.infer` `unify_generics` binds first-wins and never compares a later actual (fix in #14037), and v2 `v2.compiler.infer` `infer_match_generic_formal`. Not separately measured: whether the `==` Equivalence judgment (#13938) also waives the type-argument check for `ConstructorTag<T>` against `ConstructorTag<U>`. RUNG HONESTY (review 80322): the enrolled probe is a POLARITY-red, not a discriminating RED on the acceptance path. It asserts a runtime value (`true`) that the correct runtime answer (`false`: distinct discriminant names) contradicts, so it reads red while the defect (the accepted cross-coproduct binding) is present, but it does not itself witness the acceptance, and once infer refuses the call it stops compiling rather than greening. The authorable RED is an expect-typecheck-refusal fixture handed to the compiler; PreVerdictCause has no arm for this class and the pre-verdict admission arm is not a harness for 'must be refused', so that harness is the missing capability and this class is held at its current rung until it exists. Variant names are corpus-global, so a same-arm-name variant of the probe cannot be written. CEILING: structurally guaranteed, the checker refusing a call where one type variable resolves to two distinct declarations. NEXT TRIGGER: the infer fix, scoped as its own lane by the debt-paydown parent because it touches load-bearing stages; the constructor-tag carrier work is gated on it through its RED (iii).",
    "NOT SETTLED: whether the emitted Rust for a phantom `ConstructorTag<T>` builds (rustc E0392, unused type parameter). The standalone rustc check could not resolve the emitted crate's own imports, so it neither confirmed nor excluded E0392.",
    "RESOLVED FOR THE SHIPPED SHAPE (executed 2026-10-19, remote runner, head 945e9133f8a, `gunbc run --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 --claim-run`): the E0392 question does not reach the model that shipped. std declares `type ConstructorTag<T> sole_constructor { accepts: fn(T) -> Bool }` (std.primitives), so T occurs in the member's own type and is never an unused parameter; the carrier built and ran on both routes. A probe that spelled T as genuinely unused (a carrier whose only member was an Int) also built and ran, so no phantom vocabulary is needed either way. The unresolved-rustc caveat above stands only for the spelling it was written against.",
    "MEASURED, 2026-10-19, THE ONLY-EQUALITY AND NON-FORGEABILITY CLAUSES -- five refusal probes (four compile refusals and one runtime refusal, the ctor_tag_forge_argument_red evaluation NoSuchFunction), run by hand at head 3bf094808fa on the remote runner: `gunbc run --source-root dag --source-root src/v2 <file> --function <name> --claim-run`, interpreter built with `cargo build --release -p v1-compiler --bin gunbc`. They are deliberately NOT cited as `evidence` DeclarationRefs and carry NO admission row: a refusal is a compile error, so the file cannot resolve, a DeclarationRef against it could not load, and an ExpectedPass row would be a false receipt. Files, all under src/v2/test/claim/manual: ctor_tag_eq_red.dag, ctor_tag_ne_red.dag, ctor_tag_forge_red.dag, ctor_tag_forge_predicate_red.dag, ctor_tag_forge_argument_red.dag. EQUALITY: `tag_of(v: E0) == tag_of(v: E0)` refuses -- `ctor_tag_eq_red.dag:13:17: error: equality is not defined for 'ConstructorTag': member 'accepts' is function-valued, and function equality has no denotation — compare a declared identity for this type instead of '=='`; `tag_of(v: N0) != tag_of(v: N0)` refuses with the same sentence at `ctor_tag_ne_red.dag:10:17`, so `!=` does not slip past the rule. That is the accepted rule behind v1.compiler.infer.equality_admission_wall_note, which DERIVES the refusal from the resolved type (an Arrow member refuses) rather than naming a type -- exactly why it is not a convention an author can opt out of, and why no new vocabulary was needed. NON-FORGERY: a record literal outside std refuses -- `ctor_tag_forge_red.dag:19:16: error: sole_constructor type 'ConstructorTag' cannot be constructed outside its defining module`; a call to the deleted predicate-taking entry refuses -- `ctor_tag_forge_predicate_red.dag:11:16: error: function 'constructor_tag' not found in scope` (plus a companion `effect summary incomplete: call to 'constructor_tag' ... has no established callee identity`); and there is no third route to close: `tag_of` has exactly one parameter, the value being tagged, and no position accepts a predicate -- writing `tag_of(v: fn(_w) { true })` yields a tag over the FUNCTION type, not over the coproduct, and dies when the stored predicate reaches the builtin: `error: evaluating r3_tag_of_must_not_accept_a_predicate in src/v2/test/claim/manual/ctor_tag_forge_argument_red.dag`, cause `NoSuchFunction { name: \"discriminant\" }`. Recorded as measured rather than as the type-level refusal one might expect: T is free, so a function is a legal value for T, and the builtin is what rejects it.",
    "DEFINITIVE RUN, 2026-10-19, head 3bf094808fa (merged with main 5cda378342f; checked out on the remote runner from refs/remotes/origin/session/keen-pike-227, working tree clean at `git status --porcelain`): the 14 controls PASS 14/14 -- 7 through the v1 route (dag/test/claim/constructor_tag_route_probe.dag) and the same 7 through the v2 route (src/v2/test/claim/manual/constructor_tag_route_probe.dag) -- and the 2 rewired mirror witnesses PASS 2/2 (dag/test/claim/native_app_attest_arm_control.dag). The 2 cross-coproduct probes RUN and answer false, which is the unfixed #14152 behaviour this row records, not a regression control. The 3 refusal probes refuse with the three sentences quoted above. Nothing in the run was re-recorded from an earlier head; the forge was found at 945e9133f8a and closed at 89ac0550ec8, and the earlier receipts at 68a562722cf and 6ae9b3b9591 were the exploratory passes that shaped the model, and the strings they quote are the strings this run reproduced.",
    "FORGED TAG, FOUND AND CLOSED BY CONSTRUCTION, 2026-10-19 (review of head 945e9133f8a). The first shape exposed `fn constructor_tag<T>(accepts: fn(T) -> Bool) -> ConstructorTag<T>` as the elaboration entry, documented elaboration-only. It was FORGEABLE, and measured so: `constructor_tag(accepts: fn(_w) { true })` compiled and `same_arm` reported the forged tag as matching every arm of a three-arm coproduct -- PASS on a probe whose PASS is the defect. `sole_constructor` was no defence because it guards the RECORD LITERAL only, and a public function that builds the record is not a record literal; DESIGN section 4b says a brand is cosmetic until construction enforces it, and 'elaboration-only' written in a comment enforces nothing. There was also no visibility mechanism to reach for, which is why the candidate fix was not `public`: `public` is not a gate anywhere in resolve (no visibility predicate in v1.compiler.resolve), and type modifiers are dropped at parse by v1.compiler.parse drop_leading_type_modifier, which recognises only `nominal_opaque` and `sole_constructor`. So `sole_constructor` is the only construction control the language has, and it cannot police a function. FIX, by construction rather than by rule: the predicate-taking entry is DELETED. `tag_of<T>(v: T)` is the only function in the corpus that builds a ConstructorTag, its one parameter is a VALUE, and the predicate stored in the carrier is written once inside dag/std/primitives.dag -- so there is no parameter a caller can put a predicate in, and no entry a caller can pass a truth function to. The three NON-FORGERY diagnostics above are that fix's REDs, one per route. A tag now denotes an arm identity resolved against T's declared roster and nothing else.",
    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, 2026-10-19: THERE IS NO HARNESS THAT CAN HOLD A 'MUST REFUSE AT COMPILE' CLAIM. All five refusal probes above are prose receipts (four are compile errors; the fifth is a runtime refusal that still cannot be enrolled as an expected verdict): the file cannot resolve, so a DeclarationRef against it cannot load and an ExpectedPass admission row would assert a verdict the file never gives. The corpus has no PreVerdictCause for 'this file must fail to compile' (the causes are UnresolvedModuleOutsideClosure, ExpectAssertionFalse, ExpectTypedPreVerdictRefusal, the frozen-baseline and cadence arms), and QuarantineProbeExpectRed rows are admitted as DATA and never executed (gunbc.rung_drop.transitional_admission_exception, whose own exit condition is that the QuarantineProbeExpectRed batch gets a scheduled executor). So a refusal is currently un-witnessable: it can be described, not enrolled. The rung is a harness that takes a file plus the diagnostic it must produce and FAILS when the file compiles or when the message drifts -- until then the equality and non-forgeability clauses rest on hand-run strings, which is mitigatable, not enforced.",
    "NAMED FRONTIER, 2026-10-19, ON THE TAG ITSELF -- naming a PAYLOAD arm as a value. `same_arm` is the one exposed comparison and it takes its arms as VALUES, which works generically for a UNIT arm (`same_arm(tag: tag_of(v: W04), v: W08)` passes on both routes). A payload arm cannot be named at all: `tag_of(v: P1)` on `type Pt = P0 | P1 { a: Int }` refuses at resolve -- `src/v2/test/claim/manual/ctor_tag_arm_naming_probe.dag:13:23: error: missing required field 'a' in literal of type 'P1'` -- and the qualified spelling `Pq::Q1` never parses: `src/v2/test/claim/manual/ctor_tag_arm_naming_qualified.dag:10:25: error: expected RParen, found Colon`, surfaced fail-closed as `reference_closure: ... has no readable reference set`. Only the first repro is retained in the tree (src/v2/test/claim/manual/ctor_tag_arm_naming_probe.dag), and deliberately not as an `evidence` ref, for the reason given above. The qualified spelling is NOT retained as a file, and that is itself a rung fact: the population parse walks every module under both trees and refuses any that does not PARSE unless it is listed in gunbc.parse_population_refusals, and that list is currently empty by design (its one class was retired by the capability its trigger names). A resolve-time refusal can live in the corpus because the file still parses; a parse-time refusal cannot live anywhere without joining a list whose rule says it may only shrink. So `Pq::Q1` is recorded as measured -- `src/v2/test/claim/manual/ctor_tag_arm_naming_qualified.dag:10:25: error: expected RParen, found Colon`, surfaced fail-closed as `reference_closure: src/v2/test/claim/manual/ctor_tag_arm_naming_qualified.dag has no readable reference set` -- from a hand run at head 7f070b07785, with no file behind it. v2-bootstrap caught exactly this at 7f070b07785 by refusing `v2:test/claim/manual/ctor_tag_arm_naming_qualified.dag: parse_g0_tokens_remain`, which is the enforcement that makes the claim trustworthy rather than the transcription. The remaining idiom is the tag reference `discriminant(v: P1 {})`, and it is granted only when the callee IS `discriminant` -- v1.compiler.infer `payload_arm_tag_reference_waiver_results`, keyed on `func_name == \"discriminant\"` -- so routing a user function through the builtin does not reach it; measured, not inferred. TRIGGER: a surface for naming a payload arm as a value. The syntax is not ours to invent. UNTIL IT LANDS, a payload arm's tag comes from a value of that arm, and the agreement the `x is V` sugar depends on was pinned by the control `payload_arm_tag_reference_agrees_with_value_tag`, which compared the hollow tag reference `discriminant(v: W11 {})` against the tag of a value of that arm and passed; that control is REMOVED from both constructor_tag_route_probe modules (a hollow value is refused outside the nine listed target_model arms, gunbc.rung_drop payload_arm_tag_reference_hollow_value) and is restored when the resolved-arm capability lands. `tag_of` is the only entry the sugar calls -- see the FORGED TAG receipt; there is no separate elaboration constructor.",
    "NAMED FRONTIER, 2026-10-19, `discriminant` ADMITS ANY T -- it has no coproduct bound. Its registered signature is `BuiltinSignature { params: [BuiltinParam { name: \"v\", ty: AlgebraTypeVariable { id: \"discriminant_subject\" } }], returns: string_type }` (src/v1/04_method.dag:330), so the argument is a bare type variable and NOTHING in typing says T must be a coproduct. Consequence measured on the tag: `tag_of(v: fn(_w) { true })` passes typecheck, builds a ConstructorTag over the FUNCTION type, and only dies at evaluation inside the builtin -- `error: evaluating r3_tag_of_must_not_accept_a_predicate in src/v2/test/claim/manual/ctor_tag_forge_argument_red.dag`, cause `NoSuchFunction { name: \"discriminant\" }`. A type error surfacing as a runtime NoSuchFunction is its own defect, distinct from the tag and distinct from the payload-arm gap: any `discriminant(v: x)` call whose T is not a coproduct is accepted and fails at run time. NOT FIXED HERE. TRIGGER: a coproduct bound on `discriminant`'s type variable, so a non-coproduct argument refuses at typecheck; the two probes that today reach evaluation (`ctor_tag_forge_argument_red.dag`) then refuse earlier, and this row's claim that a tag denotes an arm of T becomes enforced at typing rather than inherited from how the builtin happens to lower.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "test.claim.constructor_tag_route_probe_red", decl_name: "same_arm_one_t_two_coproducts_must_refuse", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.test.claim.manual.constructor_tag_route_probe_red", decl_name: "same_arm_one_t_two_coproducts_must_refuse", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
